  Second question — how [38;5;153mapp.js[39m gets the settings, since the browser side has no module system today:

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether the page must keep working when you open [38;5;153mindex.html[39m straight off disk ([38;5;153mfile://[39m). ES modules are
  blocked by CORS on [38;5;153mfile://[39m; classic scripts aren't. This decides the module's export style, so it's the one worth
  getting right now — the environment map itself is identical either way.

  - [1mES modules[0m — [38;5;153msettings.js[39m uses [38;5;153mexport const[39m, [38;5;153mapp.js[39m uses [38;5;153mimport[39m, and [38;5;153mindex.html[39m becomes [38;5;153m<script type="module" [39m
    [38;5;153msrc="app.js">[39m. A real, explicit dependency with no globals, and the modern default. Cost: you must serve the folder
    over HTTP ([38;5;153mnpx serve[39m, [38;5;153mpython3 -m http.server[39m); double-clicking [38;5;153mindex.html[39m will break.
  - [1mClassic script + global[0m — [38;5;153msettings.js[39m assigns [38;5;153mwindow.APP_SETTINGS[39m, added as a second [38;5;153m<script>[39m tag before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m.
    Keeps [38;5;153mfile://[39m working and needs zero tooling. Cost: config arrives via a global rather than an import, and load
    order in [38;5;153mindex.html[39m becomes load-bearing.

  CommonJS (matching [38;5;153msrc/[39m) isn't on the table — browsers can't [38;5;153mrequire[39m without a bundler, and this repo has no build
  step.

  [1mMy recommendation: ES modules[0m, if you're willing to serve the page. The explicit import is what makes the settings
  module a real boundary rather than a global that happens to be defined first, and it's the direction this file would
  go anyway. If [38;5;153mfile://[39m convenience matters, the classic-script version delivers the same environment map with a smaller
  blast radius.
